Additional workarounds for stdexec with nvcc 12.8 and a GCC 13 host compiler.

The original nvcc workarounds allow parsing HPX's execution headers, but sender
instantiations still fail in nvcc's generated host code. This incremental patch
addresses callable non-type template parameters, unintended cv/ref qualifiers,
GCC copy elision, and references to constexpr global function objects from
host/device functions. A dependent sync_wait alias is spelled through a helper
to preserve its result type in generated host code.

Use STDEXEC_DEMANGLE_SENDER_NAMES consistently in HPX and its consumers; it
selects a different sender representation. HPX's Stdexec interface target
exports this definition for nvcc builds. These workarounds enable host-side
sender machinery in CUDA translation units, not arbitrary GPU sender execution.

With the complete patch stack, nvcc 12.8.93 and GCC 13.3 crash compiling
sync_wait(just(42)) without STDEXEC_DEMANGLE_SENDER_NAMES. The macro selects a
descriptor object instead of a lambda descriptor and must not differ between
C++ and CUDA consumers.

Per-header rationale:

- __basic_sender.hpp, __receivers.hpp, and __sender_adaptor_closure.hpp:
  construct stateless callable objects locally because references to the
  corresponding constexpr global objects are rejected in device code.
- __meta.hpp and __sender_concepts.hpp: remove unintended cv/ref qualifiers
  during completion deduction.
- __utility.hpp: avoid reference callable template arguments in generated host
  code.
- __sync_wait.hpp: keep the dependent value_type lookup valid in generated
  host code.
- __config.hpp: enable the GCC copy-elision workaround when GCC is the host
  compiler behind nvcc. See https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=98995.

Tracking: https://github.com/TheHPXProject/hpx/pull/7711. Retest the
stdexec_cuda and algorithm_sender_cuda regressions when updating the pin.

This patch applies to stdexec commit
1d58877ad898fa3422658d47b28f67deb9c10e80.

